  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how warming may reorganize butterfly communities along an elevational gradient on a mountain. In the current climate, lower elevations contain a mix of species with different heat tolerances, shown as butterflies grouped by relatively low or high thermal limits, while higher elevations are cooler and support a more restricted set of species.\n\nUnder a warmer climate, the composition at low elevation appears to change substantially. Some species shift uphill, some local species disappear, and at least one new species moves into the lowland community. This suggests turnover in which warming filters species according to their thermal tolerance and redistributes them across the mountain.\n\nAt higher elevation, the later community includes species that are now found at both low and high elevations, implying uphill expansion from warmer lowlands into formerly cooler habitats. Overall, the figure conveys a climate-driven range shift: warming reduces the distinctness of communities across elevation, causes losses and replacements at lower sites, and allows heat-tolerant species to colonize higher ground. The mountain backdrop and simple species icons emphasize the main trend of biodiversity reshuffling rather than detailed measurements.",
